demux: keep the first new packet after a refresh seek

Subtitle lines went missing in Plezy, most often the first line after the
opening minute of an episode, and only on some plays (edde746/plezy#2717).
Seeking back within the cache did not bring the line back; switching to
another subtitle track and back, then seeking, did.

A refresh seek re-reads the file from before the current read position. It
runs after a track switch, and when a seek joins a new cache range to an
existing one ("ranges joined!") and reading resumes from the end of the
cached data. Streams that were already selected drop the re-read packets
until they reach the position they had before, last_dts or last_pos. The
packet that ends that search was dropped as well, even when it lay past the
old position and was new. Video and audio read their old last packet back,
so for them that drop removes a duplicate. A subtitle stream usually has no
packet between the seek target and its old position, so the first packet it
reads back is the next subtitle line, and that line was never queued. It
never reached the cache either, which is why seeking back within the cache
could not show it.

Drop only the packets at or before the old position. Packet DTS and pos are
strictly increasing whenever the refresh uses them (correct_dts,
correct_pos), so "at or before" matches the old packet exactly. mpv master
has the same code.

diff --git a/demux/demux.c b/demux/demux.c
index d1accba..dc36193 100644
--- a/demux/demux.c
+++ b/demux/demux.c
@@ -2027,17 +2027,21 @@ static void add_packet_locked(struct sh_stream *stream, demux_packet_t *dp)
 
     if (!drop && ds->refreshing) {
         // Resume reading once the old position was reached (i.e. we start
-        // returning packets where we left off before the refresh).
-        // If it's the same position, drop, but continue normally next time.
+        // returning packets where we left off before the refresh). Drop the
+        // packet at the old position, but keep the first one past it: a
+        // sparse stream (subtitles) often has no packet between the seek
+        // target and the old position, so its first packet is already new.
         if (queue->correct_dts) {
             ds->refreshing = dp->dts < queue->last_dts;
+            drop = dp->dts <= queue->last_dts;
         } else if (queue->correct_pos) {
             ds->refreshing = dp->pos < queue->last_pos;
+            drop = dp->pos <= queue->last_pos;
         } else {
             ds->refreshing = false; // should not happen
             MP_WARN(in, "stream %d: demux refreshing failed\n", ds->index);
+            drop = true;
         }
-        drop = true;
     }
 
     if (drop) {
